ONE REVIEW. Adversarial. Do not fix anything. Answer in under 450 words.

Review an UNCOMMITTED change to a production ocean driver. It is in this
worktree's working tree (uncommitted), so read the files directly:
  scripts/run/run_omip_core2.py
  tests/ocean/unit/test_surface_flux_accumulator.py

WHAT IT DOES AND WHY. legoESM runs OMIP CORE-II against NEMO ORCA1. A snapshot
pair gives the state change but not the boundary fluxes that drove it, so no
heat budget can be closed from snapshots -- omip_conservation_closure.py says
so and refuses to fake the term. New flag --flux-accumulate adds
_SurfaceFluxAccumulator: device-side running sums of q_net, sw_down, tau_x,
tau_y taken from the OceanSurfaceForcing HANDED TO THE STEP, emitted as the
window mean into the existing `extra` snapshot channel as q_net_mean etc. plus
flux_mean_n_steps. A helper _snapshot_extra merges it with the pre-existing
--kprofile-snapshots dict. The flag is added to _RESTART_FP_EXCLUDE (a LOCAL
frozenset inside main()) because a read-only diagnostic that gets hashed into
the restart fingerprint makes a chained leg un-resumable -- this driver already
lost a two-day control that way.

It is wired at two step sites: the cubed_sphere branch (model.step) and the
tripole/latlon/MPAS branch (_ocean_step, after blend_ice_ocean_forcing, so the
accumulated flux is the ice-partitioned one the ocean actually receives).

9 tests pass.

ATTACK THESE SPECIFICALLY:

(a) IS THE ACCUMULATED QUANTITY THE ONE A HEAT BUDGET NEEDS? The intent is the
    APPLIED ocean surface heat flux. Does sf.q_net at the step call site
    actually equal what the ocean model applies, or does the model derive its
    own top-cell heating from sw_down plus penetration optics (see _q_open_top
    and shortwave_penetration) so that q_net alone double-counts or misses the
    penetrative part? Name what a budget built on these keys would get wrong.

(b) TIMING. The add() happens immediately BEFORE the step. Is sf at that point
    final on every path, or can a later _replace mutate it after the add for
    some configuration? Check the ice branch and the freshwater branch.

(c) SHARDING AND MEMORY. Sums are jnp arrays held across the whole window. On
    the persistent SPMD lane the state is sharded and gathered only at output
    boundaries. Does holding a device-resident running sum across steps break
    sharding, donation, or retrace the step? Does np.asarray in drain() force a
    gather that could fail or silently give a per-rank partial result under MPI?

(d) THE WINDOW. drain() is called from the snapshot writer. Snapshots are
    written at a day cadence AND at year boundaries AND at run end AND on
    blowup. Which of those drain and which do not, and can a window therefore
    span an unintended interval or be double-counted? Is flux_mean_n_steps
    sufficient to detect that from the file alone?

(e) TEST QUALITY. Which of the 9 tests would still pass if the feature were
    reverted? Is the restart-fingerprint source-scraping test sound, or can it
    pass vacuously?

Say what you would REJECT and the single highest-value fix.
